cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below. All thermoelectric parameters are SYNTHETIC and no hardware experiment was performed; residual change contributes zero work and no free-energy claim is made. No release or DOI exists yet: cite the repository and commit."
title: "residual-gradient-workbench: a browser workbench separating an OES-32 residual replay from a classical thermoelectric generator model"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Brisson"
    given-names: "Jean-François"
    affiliation: "Spark AI NLP"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-9778-5374"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sparkainlp-x/residual-gradient-workbench"
url: "https://sparkainlp-x.github.io/residual-gradient-workbench/"
license: AGPL-3.0-only
version: "0.1.0"
# Prepared for v0.1.0. Add date-released and the Zenodo DOIs only when the release actually exists.
abstract: >-
  Dependency-free browser prototype with two strictly separate lanes. Lane 01
  replays the normative OES-32 residual (r_i = |y_i - x_i|, R = max r_i;
  oes32-residual @ b77b612) and applies one capped correction to the first
  worst component; a falling R is labelled a simulation and the work from the
  residual is always zero. Lane 02 is a classical linear Thevenin model of a
  thermoelectric generator driven by explicit hot and cold reservoir
  temperatures; predicted output requires an external gradient, and the Carnot
  fraction is shown as a ceiling only. Default parameters are synthetic; no
  hardware experiment was performed.
keywords:
  - synthetic
  - thermoelectric
  - thevenin model
  - residual
  - OES-32
  - education
  - reproducibility
  - research software
  - javascript
